demy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-78681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/25503e83f9a196cab4ded2fd93b09028b1608bd7d815edd538564e1aa611ca86.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-78681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e404b38bbdf5c756f675cf0dfcc2c4906bcd2d62b1b4d277b8fc0cfb85e9b2d4"} {"question" "q-78681" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-78681" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-78681" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e404b38bbdf5c756f675cf0dfcc2c4906bcd2d62b1b4d277b8fc0cfb85e9b2d4"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-321989" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-275313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-275313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-275313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e404b38bbdf5c756f675cf0dfcc2c4906bcd2d62b1b4d277b8fc0cfb85e9b2d4"} {"question" "q-275313" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "d2a3396cb4e847fa3bfea1daa1813b4606dcb73fa332be0ace639c8e9e6850c1"} {"question" "q-275313" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "